Q: How can I change VSCode quick fix generated imports for TypeScript to use relative paths? A: <p>In your Visual Studio Code settings, add the following setting:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;typescript.preferences.importModuleSpecifier&quot;: &quot;relative&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: Error message &quot;error: expected color but got (raw string) color/gray&quot; A: <p>The color value should be a hexadecimal color code, like #A5D3F4 <em>or</em> a color reference with an <code>@</code> sign, like <code>@color/colorPrimary</code>.</p>
<p>Before using these reference, you must create resources, like file <em>res/values/color.xml</em>.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;?&gt;
&lt;resources&gt;
    &lt;color name=&quot;colorPrimary&quot;&gt;#6200EE&lt;/color&gt;
    &lt;color name=&quot;colorPrimaryDark&quot;&gt;#3700B3&lt;/color&gt;
    &lt;color name=&quot;colorAccent&quot;&gt;#03DAC5&lt;/color&gt;
    &lt;color name=&quot;LabelColor_text_50&quot;&gt;#FFFFFF&lt;/color&gt;
    &lt;color name=&quot;ValueColor_text_50&quot;&gt;#333322&lt;/color&gt;
    &lt;color name=&quot;LabelColor_bg_50&quot;&gt;#A5D3F4&lt;/color&gt;
    &lt;color name=&quot;ValueColor_bg_50&quot;&gt;#E6EEF4&lt;/color&gt;
&lt;/resources&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And use these names as <code>&lt;item name=&quot;someName&quot;&gt;@color/LabelColor_text_50&lt;/item&gt;</code>.</p>

Q: Finding all divisors of integer besides the int itself and 1, and putting result into array A: <p>Try this:</p>
<pre><code>function divisors(integer) {
var divisors = [];
for (var i = 2; i &lt; integer - 1; i++){
    if (integer % i === 0) divisors.push(i);
}
if (divisors.length === 0) return '' + integer + ' is prime';
return divisors;
</code></pre>
<p>}</p>

Q: Join a dataframe with multiple dataframes stored in a list with different column A: <p>I would use <code>purrr::reduce()</code> instead of <code>map()</code>. But this raises the issue that after the first iteration, <code>X</code> appears in both dataframes and is treated as a key. One workaround would be to give all the <code>X</code> columns unique names, then coalesce after joining.</p>
<pre><code>library(dplyr)
library(purrr)

test2 &lt;- imap(test, ~ rename(.x, &quot;X{.y}&quot; := X))

test2 %&gt;% 
  reduce(full_join, .init = df1) %&gt;% 
  mutate(X = coalesce(X1, X2), .keep = &quot;unused&quot;)
</code></pre>
<pre><code>   Fruits    A     B     C    D    X
1   Apple 10.0  1.30    NA   NA   oh
2  Orange  0.2    NA  0.21   NA &lt;NA&gt;
3   Grape   NA  0.06 51.00 0.70 &lt;NA&gt;
4   Grape   NA  0.06 51.00 0.70 &lt;NA&gt;
5   Grape  1.0  0.06 51.00 0.70 &lt;NA&gt;
6   Grape   NA  0.06    NA 0.80 &lt;NA&gt;
7   Berry 11.0 20.00  0.30 0.04  duh
8   Apple   NA  1.10  0.50   NA &lt;NA&gt;
9   Apple   NA  1.20  0.50   NA &lt;NA&gt;
10  Apple   NA  1.30  0.10   NA &lt;NA&gt;
11  Berry   NA    NA  0.30 0.04 &lt;NA&gt;
12  Berry  1.0    NA  0.90 0.01 &lt;NA&gt;
13  Apple  1.0  1.30  0.50   NA &lt;NA&gt;
14  Apple  1.0  1.30  0.50   NA &lt;NA&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If <code>test</code> has a lot of elements, it'll be annoying to list out <code>coalesce(X1, X2, X3, ..., Xn)</code>. In that case, you can use this alternative:</p>
<pre><code>test %&gt;%
  imap(~ rename(.x, &quot;X{.y}&quot; := X))
  reduce(full_join, .init = df1) %&gt;% 
  mutate(X = coalesce(!!!syms(paste0(&quot;X&quot;, seq_along(test2)))), .keep = &quot;unused&quot;)
</code></pre>
